  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study is based on a single qualitative case study of one infrastructure project in Kenya, which may limit its direct applicability to different institutional or regional contexts.",
      "what": "This paper analyzes the temporal dynamics of unrealized infrastructure projects using the case study of the unbuilt Lamu Coal Power Plant in Kenya, which is part of the LAPSSET corridor. It develops a three-part analytical framework that examines the historical conditions of racial capitalism and regional neglect, the contested phases of project planning and contestation, and the lived uncertainty experienced by local communities navigating an abandoned yet not fully canceled project.",
      "why": "It conceptualizes the 'unbuilt' not as a simple failure or void, but as an active state of suspension that shapes local realities, demonstrating how delayed or stalled mega-projects continue to exert social and political influence over communities."
    }
